 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Two current-limit modes",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "With VOUT above 1 V an overload is held at the programmed continuous limit, which is the more accurate of the two modes but reacts more slowly. With VOUT below 1 V the part switches to short-circuit limiting and pulses the output current at about 30 % above the continuous limit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Latch-off and auto-restart",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "If either current-limit mode lasts 20 ms (typ), FAULT goes low. With ENRESET high the output then latches off and a 25 mA source drives OUT; once OUT climbs above 0.5 V and stays there 20 ms, the switch turns back on, FAULT releases and the 25 mA source stops. Loads are assumed to draw no meaningful current below 0.5 V. With ENRESET low the switch keeps supplying the limit current for as long as the fault lasts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse current",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "While on, if current flows from OUT back to IN above 1 A (typ) for more than 20 ms (typ), the switch opens and FAULT goes low. When disabled, OUT is high impedance and current cannot flow from the output back into the input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable and undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SEL picks the active level of ON (Table 1). Below 2.5 V (typ) on IN the part stays off, and FAULT is held low through the lockout."
  },
  {
   "topic": "FAULT flag and blanking",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "FAULT is open drain; a 100 kΩ pull-up to IN suits most designs. It goes low for input below UVLO, junction temperature above +160 °C, either limit mode lasting past the 20 ms blanking time, or reverse current above 1 A lasting past the blanking time. Blanking applies to the two current-limit faults alone: thermal overload and UVLO turn the output off and pull FAULT low at once. A load that is brought up inside the 20 ms window, such as one with extra bypass capacitance or a high inrush, raises no fault. With auto-restart on, FAULT releases 20 ms after the fault clears; with it off, the output is checked every 20 ms and FAULT releases up to 20 ms after the fault clears. The input bypass capacitance must keep glitches from tripping FAULT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Above +160 °C die temperature the switch opens and FAULT goes low, with no blanking delay. After the junction cools by 15 °C the switch closes and FAULT goes high again, so a lasting overload produces a pulsed output with a lower average load."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-on and turn-off",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The switch turns on and off slowly to limit transients on the upstream supply. The output is enabled when ON is at the same logic level as SEL and disabled, high impedance, when it is at the opposite level."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "IN needs a 1 µF ceramic capacitor to ground for local decoupling; a larger value further reduces the input dip on output load steps. Inductive loads may need more capacitance to keep spikes within the absolute maximum ratings."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output capacitor",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Up to 500 µF on OUT is allowed. More can be used, but charging it at start-up may take longer than the blanking period and set FAULT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inductive loads",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "USB devices on cables add inductance that makes the port voltage ring on load steps. The part can drive such loads within its absolute maximum ratings; the load inductance is usually small, and bulk capacitance upstream plus local bypass capacitors limit overshoot. If ringing is severe, clamp OUT between -0.3 V and +6 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout and heat",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Keep input and output traces short to reduce parasitic inductance and speed the response to an output short. Place the input and output capacitors within 5 mm of the device leads. Wide power planes on IN and OUT also spread heat. Join GND to the exposed pad, and solder the pad to a broad ground plane."
  },
  {
   "topic": "USB and ESD",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part meets the IEC requirements for USB ports, and has 15 kV ESD protection with its capacitors fitted."
  }
 ],
